{
 "title": "BART and Caltrain operating costs and service",
 "description": "Operating expenses, boardings and service delivered on both main lines from FY2015 through FY2025.",
 "classification": "Operating performance context",
 "metrics": [
  {
   "label": "Operating expense",
   "field": "opex",
   "unit": "nominal dollars"
  },
  {
   "label": "Operating expense per boarding",
   "field": "nominal_cost_per_boardings",
   "unit": "nominal dollars per unlinked passenger trip"
  },
  {
   "label": "Passenger boardings",
   "field": "boardings",
   "unit": "unlinked passenger trips"
  },
  {
   "label": "Passenger-car revenue miles",
   "field": "revenue_car_miles",
   "unit": "car-miles"
  },
  {
   "label": "Passenger-car revenue hours",
   "field": "revenue_car_hours",
   "unit": "car-hours"
  },
  {
   "label": "Train revenue miles",
   "field": "train_revenue_miles",
   "unit": "train-miles, FY2022 onward in this comparison"
  }
 ],
 "notes": [
  {
   "title": "What happened to the cost of carrying a rider?",
   "text": "On BART heavy rail, FY2019 operating expense of $651.0 million supported 125.1 million boardings. In FY2025 it spent $885.6 million for 56.6 million boardings. Cost per boarding rose from $5.20 to $15.65. Caltrain spent $136.3 million for 17.7 million boardings in FY2019 and $218.8 million for 11.0 million in FY2025, moving from $7.71 to $19.87 per boarding.",
   "sources": [
    "ntd",
    "opex"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Why Caltrain publishes two annual ridership estimates",
   "text": "Caltrain estimated 9.1 million FY2025 trips from fare media. The federal database reports 11,011,874 FY2025 commuter-rail boardings from sampled ride checks. Caltrain explains that the fare model omits trips without a valid fare and estimates usage of passes. In January 2025 it raised its monthly-pass assumption from 26 to 37 trips, following a rider survey. The agency reported 12.6 million FY2026 trips and 37.6% growth using its own fare-media series. The federal series supplies every cost-per-boarding denominator in this comparison.",
   "sources": [
    "caltrain",
    "caltrain26",
    "ntd",
    "annual"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Demand collapsed faster than train service",
   "text": "FY2021 was the pandemic trough for both main lines. Cost per boarding reached $35.69 on BART and $106.67 on Caltrain. Both figures then fell as riders returned. Some service costs continue when fewer people board a train. BART’s triennial auditor cautioned that the pandemic disrupted prior performance trends.",
   "sources": [
    "ntd",
    "audit"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "How much service did the agencies run?",
   "text": "From FY2019 to FY2025, BART heavy-rail passenger-car hours in revenue service fell 2.6%. Caltrain’s rose 36.9%. After adjusting for the local consumer price index, cost per revenue car-hour rose 15.6% on BART and fell 3.0% on Caltrain. These ratios describe the cost of supplying a car-hour under each agency’s service model.",
   "sources": [
    "ntd",
    "annual",
    "cpi"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "A train-mile and a car-mile tell different stories",
   "text": "A train with several passenger cars produces one train-mile for each mile it runs and one car-mile per car. In FY2025 BART ran 0.7% more train-miles than in FY2024 while its car-miles fell 5.7%. BART had shortened trains and revised its schedule to improve evening and weekend frequencies. Caltrain began electric mainline service in September 2024 and increased weekday service to stations. Its train-miles grew 10.9%, car-miles grew 71.5% and boardings grew 48.7%. The number of car-miles alone cannot establish the change in train frequency.",
   "sources": [
    "ntd",
    "annual",
    "bart",
    "caltrain",
    "review"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Which expense categories grew?",
   "text": "Between FY2019 and FY2025, BART heavy-rail general administration rose 75.6% in nominal dollars. Caltrain commuter-rail facility maintenance rose 150.8% and general administration rose 91.7%. The federal tables classify spending by function. They do not break these changes into wages, allocation changes, asset maintenance, and contract pricing across both endpoint years.",
   "sources": [
    "ntd",
    "opex"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "What the efficiency review found",
   "text": "The MTC review credited BART with $516 million and Caltrain with more than $76 million in cumulative operating savings over FY2020–25. These are estimated savings from specific measures across multiple years. They should not be subtracted from a single year’s federal operating figure. This comparison does not establish an improper payment or a recoverable loss.",
   "sources": [
    "review",
    "adopt"
   ]
  }
 ],
 "method": [
  "BART is National Transit Database agency 90003, heavy rail (HR), directly operated (DO). Caltrain is agency 90134, commuter rail (CR), purchased transportation (PT). Other BART and Caltrain modes are excluded from these mainline figures.",
  "Every year is a fiscal year ending June 30. Operating expenses are dollars actually reported in the federal operating expense series. Capital construction budgets, including Caltrain’s electrification project budget, are outside this comparison.",
  "A boarding is an unlinked passenger trip. A transfer can create another boarding. A passenger mile measures how far passengers ride. Revenue car-miles and car-hours describe cars operating in passenger service. Train-miles describe train movements. Route or track miles measure network length and are absent from these calculations.",
  "Real-dollar values use the mean of all six published observations in each July–June period for the BLS San Francisco–Oakland–Hayward all-items CPI-U, with FY2019 as the reference year. It measures local purchasing power. It does not measure the particular mix of prices, labor and contracts faced by a train operator. The downloaded CPI series does not fully cover FY2015–18; those cells are blank.",
  "Twenty-two mainline rows cover FY2015–25. All 748 freshly downloaded federal rows matched the earlier two-agency extract. The two FY2025 operating totals and all overlapping FY2022–25 annual boardings, passenger miles, revenue car-hours and revenue car-miles matched the separate annual tables."
 ],
